+
+# Physical decoder placement
+
+A load names an ordered set of distinct physical devices. Contiguous decoder
+stages are assigned from the artifact's semantic weight roles and state
+descriptors. Entry, readout and the drafter belong to the first device. Each
+stage must fit its own stable capacity, including its exact resident weights,
+history, banks, graph resources, boundary buffers and overlapping startup
+storage. A partition minimizes the greatest physical occupancy among feasible
+cuts. Shared history constrains cuts: a reader and its source stay together.
+No model-family name participates in placement or execution.
+
+One conversation owns one logical state transaction across all stages. Physical
+history domains are grouped by owner. Recurrent components preserve their
+original indices and common bank IDs while each owner backs its own slabs.
+Growth succeeds on every required owner before logical backing is published;
+a failed growth rolls back unpublished physical allocations. Relocation copies
+all owners before publishing a new bank placement. Cancellation or a failed
+stage publishes no accepted successor and drains already submitted work.
+
+Each native graph and every binding retain their physical device. A boundary
+waits for its producer and transports the exact declared hidden-row bytes into
+receiver-owned storage before submission. The original graph binding checks
+continue to reject foreign-device tensors. Transfers report physical endpoints,
+payload bytes and elapsed time. Weights, history codecs, activation rounding,
+intermediate precision and target verification semantics are unchanged.
+
+Scheduling remains the shared session scheduler: it bounds launch rows and
+in-flight state, preserves per-session isolation and admits growth only when
+all affected physical owners can back it. Adding devices does not create
+independent conversation commits or exclusive per-device sessions. Acceptance
+requires loading the original artifact on the selected devices and real
+concurrent Pi tool loops; planner or transfer fixtures alone are insufficient.
